TI  - Roles of BrlA and AbaA in Mediating Asexual and Insect Pathogenic Lifecycles of Metarhizium robertsii.
LID - 10.3390/jof8101110 [doi]
LID - 1110
AB  - BrlA and AbaA are key activators of the central developmental pathway (CDP) that controls asexual development in Aspergillus but their roles remain insufficiently understood in hypocerealean insect pathogens. Here, regulatory roles of BrlA and AbaA orthologs in Metarhizium robertsii (Clavicipitaceae) were characterized for comparison to those elucidated previously in Beauveria bassiana (Cordycipitaceae) at phenotypic and transcriptomic levels. Time-course transcription profiles of brlA, abaA, and the other CDP activator gene wetA revealed that they were not so sequentially activated in M. robertsii as learned in Aspergillus. Aerial conidiation essential for fungal infection and dispersal, submerged blastospore production mimicking yeast-like budding proliferation in insect hemocoel, and insect pathogenicity via cuticular penetration were all abolished as a consequence of brlA or abaA disruption, which had little impact on normal hyphal growth. The disruptants were severely compromised in virulence via cuticle-bypassing infection (intrahemocoel injection) and differentially impaired in cellular tolerance to oxidative and cell wall-perturbing stresses. The brlA and abaA mutant shad 255 and 233 dysregulated genes (up/down ratios: 52:203 and 101:122) respectively, including 108 genes co-dysregulated. These counts were small compared with 1513 and 2869 dysregulated genes (up/down ratios: 707:806 and 1513:1356) identified in brlA and abaA mutants of B. bassiana. Results reveal
